    "cursorSync": "Off",
    "description": "Is Cuenca a 10-minute city? Every street coloured by how many of six daily needs (food, a pharmacy, a health centre, a school, a park, a stop) are within a walk of 1 to 15 minutes on a clock you drag or play, timed by R5 on the city's streets.",
    "editable": true,
    "elements": {
      "panel-about": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "",
          "id": 8,
          "links": [],
          "title": "About the data",
          "vizConfig": {
            "group": "text",
            "kind": "VizConfig",
            "spec": {
              "fieldConfig": {
                "defaults": {},
                "overrides": []
              },
              "options": {
                "code": {
                  "language": "plaintext",
                  "showLineNumbers": false,
                  "showMiniMap": false
                },
                "content": "**Play the clock.** Its window is a walk, leaving home at 7:00: drag its end anywhere from 7:01 to 7:15, or press play to grow it a minute at a time. Every street of Cuenca takes the colour of how many of six daily needs are within that walk of it, and the figures and charts follow at every step. Hover a street for the minutes to each need. After the first load nothing goes back to the server: Chaski (DuckDB-WASM) holds the walks in your browser, Mosaic filters the charts and kepler.gl the map, on your graphics card.\n\n**The walks** come from [R5](https://github.com/conveyal/r5), Conveyal's routing engine, running for Cuenca on Ubica's servers on OpenStreetMap's streets and paths, on flat ground at 1.3 m/s (4.7 km/h). R5 lays a grid of cells of about 153 m on the city and walked from every cell holding a need to every cell around it, within 15 minutes; a walk on the flat is as long either way, so that is the walk from each cell to its nearest place of each kind. A cell counts as within *m* minutes when R5 reaches it in less than *m*. The hills are not in it: with them, many of these walks would be longer uphill.\n\n**The six needs.** *Food*: corner shops, groceries, minimarkets, bakeries and food distributors from the city's trade permits, and the markets and fairs of fresh produce. *Health*: the centres, clinics and hospitals of the Ministry of Health's register. *Schools*: every level up to the end of secondary. These three are [LlactaLAB](https://llactalab.ucuenca.edu.ec)'s land uses of Cuenca (Universidad de Cuenca), a snapshot whose date its catalogue does not give, so a shop opened or closed since is not in it. *Pharmacies* join the permits' with [Overture Maps](https://overturemaps.org)'s (release 2026-10-21), as neither has them all. *Parks and squares* are LlactaLAB's 381 public parks by their entrances (reserves left out), *stops* those of the city's GTFS for its 37 bus routes and its tram.\n\n**People** are LlactaLAB's population per building (Google Open Buildings, with the population spread over them), summed per cell: 566,000 people in the area R5 covers, the city and the parishes around it. **Streets** are Overture's (from OpenStreetMap, ODbL), cut at the cells' edges. People and streets are built by Pacha's `datasets/cuenca-access/build.sh`, the needs and walks by `datasets/cuenca-walk/build.sh` in [pacha-dashboards](https://github.com/ubica-ecuador/pacha-dashboards), which needs the R5 instance of Cuenca: only pacha.ubica.dev has it.",
                "mode": "markdown"
              }
            },
            "version": "13.2.1"
          }
        }
      },
      "panel-all": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
